The observation that the NS2-5B/NCP7 WT replicon exhibited a lower replication efficiency than the NS2-5B/CP7 WT replicon in naive MDBK cells at 24 h and 48 hpe argues that the DNAJC14-mediated temporal regulation mechanism of NS2-3 cleavage during the replication of the NS2-5B/NCP7 WT replicon contributes to this lower replication efficiency and is in line with the lower RNA replication efficiency known for NCP7 infections than CP7 infections (
14
).

Our experiments using the GST-Jiv90(WT) rescue cell lines confirm that the Jiv90 domain of DNAJC14 (i.e., in the absence of the full-length protein) is also sufficient to activate the NS2 proteases of different pestiviruses (
Fig. 6
), most likely by its interaction with the two Jiv90-binding domains in NS2 (
53
). Our experiments reemphasize that, mechanistically, the cochaperone function of the DNAJC14 J-domain (essential for the association between J-domain proteins with chaperones of the Hsp70 family [
85
]) is not required for NS2 activation, as proposed earlier (
53
).
